d the rook on a1. White’s pawn structure is loose, and White’s dark-squared bishop is on d5, not defending c2. White would have to respond with a move like Qc1 or Rb1, losing tempo. Additionally, it sets up a possible discovered attack by moving the knight later. This creates immediate pressure while White is underdeveloped. It’s more aggressive and capitalizes on White’s lack of piece coordination compared to quieter moves like pawn advances or king moves.  \n\nAnswer: N: d4-c2"}
